#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Netskope Cloud Exchange pre-flight check.

Run this before installing, upgrading, or migrating Cloud Exchange to
confirm the host meets the prerequisites for a target CE version.

A single self-contained script: download it and run it directly, with
no other files, packages or dependencies required.

Run it as root, the same way setup itself is invoked:

    sudo python3 ./ce_healthcheck

This is required, not advisory -- the script exits 2 if it is not root. An
unprivileged run cannot read the management service's interpreter, and
rootless `podman ps` reports the invoking user's containers instead of
root's, which would make a healthy deployment's ports read as failures with
no indication that anything was missed.

Nothing is ever prompted for. Run context, deployment type (SA/HA) and target
version are detected from the host, and every one of them can be overridden
with a flag, so the same invocation behaves identically from a terminal, from
CI, and from the management server.

    sudo ./ce_healthcheck                      # detect everything
    sudo ./ce_healthcheck --manifest-path ./checks_bundled.json  # no outbound internet
    sudo ./ce_healthcheck --quiet              # suppress the detection notes
    sudo ./ce_healthcheck --html report.html
    sudo ./ce_healthcheck --mode upgrade --deployment HA --target-version 7.0.0

Standard library only, Python 3.6+, so it runs on any host CE supports.

The sections below, in file order:

    Result records, rendering, reports  a check's result dict, plus the
                                        console/JSON/HTML renderings of it
    Version parsing                     the PEP-440 subset CE versions use
    Guarded execution                   the only way this script shells out
                                        or reaches the network
    Host facts                          everything measured about the host,
                                        collected once up front
    Check engine                        the primitives a manifest entry may
                                        name, and what each one means
    The check manifest                  fetching and validating the check
                                        definitions
    Host detection and CLI              detect mode/deployment/version, then
                                        main()

Known limitation: this tool does not validate the CE deployment directory's
file/folder structure or permissions (e.g. data/mongo-data, data/ca_certs,
./start, ./stop). It is a pre-install/pre-upgrade check that can run before
that structure exists at all, and setup's own
check_and_apply_file_permissions() creates and repairs these paths itself
during install/upgrade. A clean pass here does not guarantee they are
present or correctly permissioned on an existing deployment.
"""

